> API and interpretation

An SCS need not be unique. The explicit first-input tie rule preserves
historical ordering. Empty inputs work; table and optional retained-history
bounds are separate. Full DP is deliberately retained rather than claiming
Hirschberg linear storage.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Hirschberg, A linear space algorithm for computing maximal common subsequences (1975), background to LCS|https://doi.org/10.1145/360825.360861]

